Three gaps in tess doctor, all in how it finds things, not in what it checks. (1) The user Claude settings file is found via $HOME; honour CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR first and fall back to ~/.claude. (2) other_trackers reads .claude/settings.json and the user file but never .claude/settings.local.json, where a personal beads hook would hide; read it too. (3) The hooks check only asks whether some tess is on PATH. Use os.Executable (hook.go already does for session-end) and compare: no tess on PATH -> warn and name the running binary directory to add; a tess on PATH that is a different file -> warn naming both paths, since the hooks will run the other one; same file -> ok. Update doctor.txtar and the README Doctor output section for the new wordings.